15.6.m:
'Divine Waters, child of the waters!
You are givers of the realm;
grant the realm, svāhā!
You are the bull-wave;
you are givers of the realm;
grant the realm, svāhā!
You are the bull-army;
you are givers of the realm;
grant the realm, svāhā!
You are the lord of the waters;
you are givers of the realm;
grant the realm, svāhā!
You are never failing;
you are overflowing;
you are full of vigor;
you dwell in the fold;
you have the luster of the sun;
you have the skin of the sun;
you are soothing;
you are subject [to our will];
you are mighty;
you are the vigor of the Maruts;
you are supporters of people;
you are supporters of all;
you are unassailable, the sap of waters and plants;
you are most famed;
you are givers of the realm;
grant the realm, svāhā!'
'I have taken the divine waters rich in sweetness, full of strength, attentive to the royal consecration (rājasūya), / with which they sprinkled Mitra and Varuṇa, with which they led Indra across his foes.'
'Divine Waters rich in sweetness, mingle together, winning great dominion for the kṣatriya! / Sit, unassailable, full of strength, bestowing great splendor upon the kṣatriya!'
'You are unsuppressed, kin of speech, born of heat, / the gift of Soma.
Bright ones, I purify you with the bright; / shining ones, I purify you with the shining.
May the divine Savitṛ purify you with an unbroken strainer, with the rays of the sun, / svāhā, you waters of the royal consecration!'
'Feasting together, splendid, unassailable, one in strength, clad in water, / Varuṇa made a dwelling in the rivers, the child of the waters, within the most motherly ones.'
'O Rudra, whatever highest name of yours is Krivi, unto that it is offered;
it is sacrificed to Yama, svāhā!
Soma, Indra, Varuṇa, Mitra, Agni—may these gods who uphold the order uphold the order; may they impel speech for him!'

15.10.m:
The bahiṣpavamāna is ninefold; the ājya [stotras] are fifteenfold;
the midday pavamāna is fifteenfold; the pṛṣṭha [stotras] are seventeenfold;
the ārbhava pavamāna is seventeenfold; the agniṣṭoma [sāman] is twenty-one-fold.
The pavamānas of the abhiṣecanīya are thirty-four-fold; the ājyas are fifteenfold;
the pṛṣṭhas are seventeenfold; the agniṣṭoma is twenty-one-fold, and the ukthas [likewise]; the entire daśapeya is seventeenfold.
The bahiṣpavamāna of the keśavapanīya is twenty-one-fold; the ājyas are seventeenfold;
the midday pavamāna is seventeenfold; the pṛṣṭhas are fifteenfold;
the ārbhava pavamāna is fifteenfold; the agniṣṭoma is ninefold, and the ukthas [likewise]; the ṣoḍaśin is twenty-one-fold; the night is fifteenfold; the sandhi is ninefold.
Then this two-night [rite] is the vyuṣṭi; as many as are the days and nights of the year, so many are its stotra-verses.

16.2.m:
Agni surveyed the front of the dawns; the first Jātavedas [surveyed] the days; / and you have stretched along the rays of the sun in many places, along heaven and earth.
Having traversed the path, the courser shakes off all assailants;
he seeks to perceive Agni in the great seat with his eye.
Stepping onto the earth, o courser, seek Agni with your luster;
having covered [it] with the ground, tell us from where we may dig him.
The sky is your back, the earth your seat, the intermediate space your breath, the ocean your womb;
having discerned with your eye, step upon the assailants.
Step up for great good fortune from this place, o wealth-giving courser;
may we be in the favor of the earth, digging Agni in her lap.
The wealth-giving courser has stepped up, near to the good realm, well made, of the earth;
thence may we dig Agni of beautiful appearance, climbing up to heaven, onto the highest firmament.
I sprinkle Agni with thought, with ghee, who dwells facing all worlds, / broad, large with transverse vigor, widest food, violent, discerning.
I sprinkle you with speech, with ghee; with mind free from demons may you enjoy that;
Agni, of youthful beauty, of desirable color, not to be assailed, bristling with his body.
The sage Agni, lord of the prize, has stepped around the oblations, / bestowing treasures upon the giver.
Around you, o sage Agni, o mighty one, we place a fortress, / of bold color day by day, the slayer of the deceitful.
You, o Agni, with days; you, desiring to shine; you from the waters; you out of the stone;
you from the trees; you from the plants; you, manly-minded, are born pure from men.

16.4.m:
Let flow the divine waters, sweet, for the health of offspring;
from their resting-place let the plants arise, bearing good fruit.
May Vāyu Mātariśvan unite your heart that has expanded, you who are spread out upward;
you who move with the breath of the gods, to that one, o god, be vaṣaṭ, to you.
Well-born with light, he has reached the sun, the shelter and armor;
put on the garment of all forms, o Agni, radiant with light.
Arise, favored by sacrifice, with divine grace toward us;
and to be seen with great light, with shining glows, o Agni, come with high praises.
Stand upright for our aid, like god Savitṛ;
upright, bestower of prize, when with anointed celebrants we call upon [you].
Born as a child of the two worlds, o Agni, lovely, distributed among the plants,
a brilliant infant anointed through the darknesses, roaring, you have gone forth from the mothers.
Be firm, strong-limbed; be swift, o courser, racer;
be broad, easy to sit upon, bearer of Agni's soil.
Be kind to human offspring, o Aṅgiras;
do not scorch heaven and earth, nor the intermediate space, nor the trees.
Let the courser go forth, whinnying, braying, flying, the donkey;
bearing Agni who is in the soil, may he not fall before his lifespan.
The bull bearing the bull Agni, the child of the waters, oceanic;
o Agni, come to the feast.
Ṛta is truth, ṛta is truth.
O plants, receive this Agni, who comes kindly here toward you;
driving away all languor and diseases, sitting down, strike away ill will from us.
O plants, rejoice, having flowers and good fruit;
this your timely child has sat down in his ancient seat.
Shining brightly with broad radiance, drive back foes, demons, and diseases;
may I be in the refuge of well-protecting, lofty Agni, easy to invoke, under his guidance.
Since the waters are joyous, bestow sap upon us,
for great and delightful sight.
Whatever is your most auspicious essence, share it with us here,
like loving mothers.
To that one we would go swiftly, to whose house you enliven [us];
and, o waters, produce [vigor] for us.

16.8.m:
Seen, the gold disc has shone out widely, shining for glory, unendurable life;
Agni became immortal through his powers when heaven, rich in seed, begot him.
Night and Dawn, like-minded, of different forms, coming together, nurse the one child;
the gold disc shines between heaven and earth; the gods, givers of wealth, maintained Agni.
The sage puts on all forms; he has impelled good fortune for two-footed and four-footed;
Savitṛ, the choice one, has looked out upon the firmament; he shines following the march of Dawn.
You are the eagle, winged;
the Trivṛt is your head;
the Gāyatra is your eye;
the Bṛhat and Rathantara are your wings;
the Stoma is your body;
the meters are your limbs;
the Yajus formulas are your name;
the Vāmadevya Sāman is your form;
the Yajñāyajñīya is your tail;
the hearth-mounds are your hooves;
you are the eagle, winged;
go to heaven;
fly to the light!
You are the stride of Viṣṇu that slays the rival;
mount the Gāyatrī meter;
stride along the earth!
You are the stride of Viṣṇu that slays the assailant;
mount the Triṣṭubh meter;
stride along the atmosphere!
You are the stride of Viṣṇu that slays the ill-wisher;
mount the Jagatī meter;
stride along heaven!
You are the stride of Viṣṇu that slays the enemy;
mount the Anuṣṭubh meter;
stride along the quarters!
Agni roared like thundering heaven, licking the earth, anointing the plants;
born instantly, kindled, he looked abroad; he shines between both worlds with his beam.
O Agni, returning one, return to us with life, with luster, with offspring, with wealth, with gain, with wisdom.
O Agni, Aṅgiras, let there be a hundred turnings to you, a thousand returns to you;
then with the thriving of prosperity bring back to us what was lost, bring back to us wealth!
Return again with vigor, return again, o Agni, with nourishment and life;
protect us again from distress!
Return together with wealth, o Agni; swell with a stream
all-nourishing from every side!
I have brought you; you have been inside; stand firm, unvacillating;
let all the clans desire you; establish sovereignty among us!
Untie the highest fetter from us, o Varuṇa, the lowest, loosen the middle one;
then may we, o Āditya, in your vow, be sinless for Aditi.
At the head of the dawns he stood high, upright, having gone forth from darkness he came with light;
Agni, with handsome limbs, with his gleaming beam, as soon as born filled all dwellings.
The goose sitting in the pure, the Vasu sitting in the atmosphere, the Hotṛ sitting at the altar, the guest sitting in the house,
sitting among men, sitting in the best [place], sitting in truth, sitting in the sky, born of water, born of kine, born of truth, born of the rock, the truth!
Sit in the lap of this mother, knowing all pathways, o Agni;
do not scorch her with heat, nor with flame; shine within her with bright light!
Within the pot, o Agni, in your own seat with luster,
heating her with heat, o Jātavedas, be kind!
Having become kind to me, o Agni, then sit down kind;
having made all quarters kind, sit here in your own womb!

16.9.m:
From heaven first Agni was born, from us the second time Jātavedas;
the third time in the waters the manly-minded one; kindling him unceasingly, the devout person begets him.
We know your three threefold forms, o Agni; we know your seat distributed in many places;
we know that spring from which you have come; we know your highest name which is in secret.
In the ocean the manly-minded one, watching over men, kindled you within the waters, o Agni, in the udder of heaven;
the buffaloes gladdened you standing in the third realm, in the womb of truth.
Agni roared like thundering heaven, licking the earth, anointing the plants;
born instantly, kindled, he looked abroad; he shines between both worlds with his beam.
Eager, purifying, the messenger, wise, Agni the immortal was set among mortals;
the ruddy one sends up smoke, bearing it repeatedly, reaching up to heaven with bright radiance.
Raiser of splendors, foundation of riches, impeller of inspired thoughts, guardian of Soma,
the Vasu, son of strength, king in the waters, shines at the head of the dawns, being kindled.
The king of the whole world was born; being born, he filled both worlds;
the rock, passing away, split even the firm [pen], when the five peoples sacrificed to Agni.
Whoever today shall make for you, o god Agni of auspicious radiance,
a cake with ghee,
lead him further forward toward greater wealth, toward god-ordained brilliance, o youngest one!
Make him share in glorious renown, o Agni; make him share in the recited hymn;
he shall be dear to the sun, dear to Agni; he shall burst forth with what is born, with what is to be born.
Seen, the gold disc has shone out widely, shining for glory, unendurable life;
Agni became immortal through his powers when heaven, rich in seed, begot him.
You, o Agni, the sacrificers have day by day taken as all good things to be chosen;
desiring wealth along with you, the eager ones opened the pen rich in kine.

16.19.m:
This here in front is Bhū [the earth];
his breath is derived from Bhū;
spring is derived from breath;
the gāyatrī belongs to spring;
from the gāyatrī comes the Gāyatra [sāman];
from the Gāyatra comes the Upāṃśu [cup];
from the Upāṃśu comes the Trivṛt [stoma];
from the Trivṛt comes the Rathantara [sāman];
Vasiṣṭha is the seer.
Through you, grasped by Prajāpati, I take breath for creatures.
This on the south is Viśvakarman;
his mind is derived from Viśvakarman;
summer is derived from mind;
the triṣṭubh belongs to summer;
from the triṣṭubh comes the Svāra [sāman];
from the Svāra comes the Antaryāma [cup];
from the Antaryāma comes the Pañcadaśa [stoma];
from the Pañcadaśa comes the Bṛhat [sāman];
Bharadvāja is the seer.
Through you, grasped by Prajāpati, I take mind for creatures.
This in the west is Viśvavyacas [all-embracing];
his eye is derived from Viśvavyacas;
the rains are derived from the eye;
the jagatī belongs to the rainy season;
from the jagatī comes the Ṛk-sama [sāman];
from the Ṛk-sama comes the Śukra [cup];
from the Śukra comes the Saptadaśa [stoma];
from the Saptadaśa comes the Vairūpa [sāman];
Jamadagni is the seer.
Through you, grasped by Prajāpati, I take the eye for creatures.
This on the north is Svar [light];
his ear is derived from Svar;
autumn is derived from the ear;
the anuṣṭubh belongs to autumn;
from the anuṣṭubh comes the Aiḍa [sāman];
from the Aiḍa comes the Manthin [cup];
from the Manthin comes the Ekaviṃśa [stoma];
from the Ekaviṃśa comes the Vairāja [sāman];
Viśvāmitra is the seer.
Through you, grasped by Prajāpati, I take the ear for creatures.
This above is Mati [thought];
her speech is derived from Mati;
winter is derived from speech;
the paṅkti belongs to winter;
from the paṅkti comes the Nidhanavat [sāman];
from the Nidhanavat comes the Āgrāyaṇa [cup];
from the Āgrāyaṇa come the Triṇava and Trayastriṃśa [stomas];
from the Triṇava and Trayastriṃśa come the Śākvara and Raivata [sāmans];
Viśvakarman is the seer.
Through you, grasped by Prajāpati, I take speech for creatures.
Fill the space, fill the gap, and then sit firm!
Indra, Agni, and Bṛhaspati have seated you in this womb.
These dappled cows, well-milking, mix the Soma
at the birth of the gods, the clans, in the three realms of heaven.

