17.8.m:
You are the queen,
the eastern quarter;
the Vasus are your divine overlords;
Agni is the resister of weapons.
May the threefold stoma support you on earth;
may the ājya uktha prop [you] for steadfastness;
[may] the Rathantara sāman [support you in] the intermediate space for firm standing.
May the first-born seers among the gods spread you out according to the measure and breadth of heaven,
and this supporter and overlord;
may all of them, in agreement, seat you and the sacrificer on the back of the firmament, in the heavenly world.
You are the ruler,
the southern quarter;
the Rudras are your divine overlords;
Indra is the resister of weapons.
May the fifteenfold stoma support you on earth;
may the prauga uktha prop [you] for steadfastness;
[may] the Bṛhat sāman [support you in] the intermediate space for firm standing.
May the first-born seers among the gods spread you out according to the measure and breadth of heaven,
and this supporter and overlord;
may all of them, in agreement, seat you and the sacrificer on the back of the firmament, in the heavenly world.
You are the sovereign,
the western quarter;
the Ādityas are your divine overlords;
Soma is the resister of weapons.
May the seventeenfold stoma support you on earth;
may the marutvatīya uktha prop [you] for steadfastness;
[may] the Vairūpa sāman [support you in] the intermediate space for firm standing.
May the first-born seers among the gods spread you out according to the measure and breadth of heaven,
and this supporter and overlord;
may all of them, in agreement, seat you and the sacrificer on the back of the firmament, in the heavenly world.
You are the autonomous ruler,
the northern quarter;
the Maruts are your divine overlords;
Varuṇa is the resister of weapons.
May the twenty-onefold stoma support you on earth;
may the niṣkevalya uktha prop [you] for steadfastness;
[may] the Vairāja sāman [support you in] the intermediate space for firm standing.
May the first-born seers among the gods spread you out according to the measure and breadth of heaven,
and this supporter and overlord;
may all of them, in agreement, seat you and the sacrificer on the back of the firmament, in the heavenly world.
You are the overlady,
the lofty quarter;
the All-Gods are your divine overlords;
Bṛhaspati is the resister of weapons.
May the twenty-sevenfold and thirty-threefold stomas support you on earth;
may the vaiśvadeva and āgnimāruta ukthas prop [you] for steadfastness;
[may] the Śākvara and Raivata sāmans [support you in] the intermediate space for firm standing.
May the first-born seers among the gods spread you out according to the measure and breadth of heaven,
and this supporter and overlord;
may all of them, in agreement, seat you and the sacrificer on the back of the firmament, in the heavenly world.

17.9.m:
This in the east is Harikeśa, the ray of the sun;
his general and troop-leader are Rathakṛtsa and Rathaujas;
Puñjikasthalā and Kṛtasthalā are his two apsarases;
sorcerers are his weapon;
monsters are his missile.
May they protect us;
may they help us;
him whom we hate and who hates us, I place in their jaws.
This in the south is Viśvakarman;
his general and troop-leader are Rathasvana and Rathecitra;
Menakā and Sahajanyā are his two apsarases;
biting beasts are his weapon;
murder by men is his missile.
May they protect us;
may they help us;
him whom we hate and who hates us, I place in their jaws.
This in the west is Vidadvasu;
his general and troop-leader are Rathaprota and Asamaratha;
Pramlocantī and Anumlocantī are his two apsarases;
tigers are his weapon;
serpents are his missile.
May they protect us;
may they help us;
him whom we hate and who hates us, I place in their jaws.
This in the north is Saṃyadvasu;
his general and troop-leader are Tārkṣya and Ariṣṭanemi;
Viśvācī and Ghṛtācī are his two apsarases;
waters are his weapon;
wind is his missile.
May they protect us;
may they help us;
him whom we hate and who hates us, I place in their jaws.
This above is Arvāgvasu;
his general and troop-leader are Senajit and Suṣeṇa;
Urvaśī and Pūrvacitti are his two apsarases;
the roaring [thunder] is his weapon;
lightning is his missile.
May they protect us;
may they help us;
him whom we hate and who hates us, I place in their jaws.

17.10.m:
I seat you in the seat of Ayu, in the shade of the well.
Homage to the sea;
homage to the eye of the sea.
Snorting like a horse longing for pasture, when he stood abroad in the great enclosure,
then the wind blows after his flame; then may your path be black.
May Prajāpati seat you on the back of the earth, full of light;
for all in-breath, out-breath, [and] diffused breath, bestow all light;
Agni is your overlord.
May Viśvakarman seat you on the back of the intermediate space, full of light;
for all in-breath, out-breath, [and] diffused breath, bestow all light;
Vāyu is your overlord.
May Parameṣṭhin seat you on the back of heaven, full of light;
for all in-breath, out-breath, [and] diffused breath, bestow all light;
Sūrya is your overlord.
Madhu and Mādhava are the two spring seasons;
you are the internal union of Agni.
May heaven and earth be fit; may waters and plants be fit;
may the fires be fit separately, united in vow for my supremacy.
The fires that are of one mind between heaven and earth,
fitting with these two spring seasons, may they enter into [me] like the gods into Indra.
Śukra and Śuci are the two summer seasons;
Nabhas and Nabhasya are the two rainy seasons;
Iṣ and Ūrj are the two autumn seasons;
Sahas and Sahasya are the two winter seasons;
Tapas and Tapasya are the two cool seasons;
you are the internal union of Agni.
May heaven and earth be fit; may waters and plants be fit;
may the fires be fit separately, united in vow for my supremacy.
The fires that are of one mind between heaven and earth,
fitting with these two cool seasons, may they enter into [me] like the gods into Indra.
You are the measure of a thousand;
you are the counterpart of a thousand;
you are the limit of a thousand;
you belong to a thousand;
for a thousand [I take] you.
May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni:
one and ten,
ten and a hundred,
a hundred and a thousand,
a thousand and ten thousand,
ten thousand and a hundred thousand,
a hundred thousand and a million,
a million and ten million,
ten million and a hundred million,
a billion and a middle,
and the end and the highest number.
May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni.
You are the seasons, increasing the order;
you are season-abiding, touching the seasons;
dripping ghee, dripping honey,
vigorous, full of the svadhā-call,
granting wishes by the name of the Virājes.
May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni.

17.16.m:
Homage to you sparkling ones, the hearts of the gods.
Homage to the discerning ones.
Homage to the destroyers.
Homage to the eradicators.
O runner, lord of food, poor one, dark blue and red,
of these offspring, of these men, of these beasts,
do not be afraid, do not harm, let not anything of ours be afflicted.
These prayers we offer to Rudra, the strong one with braided hair, ruling over heroes,
so that well-being may be for our two-footed and four-footed, [and] everything in this village may be nourished and free of disease.
That form of yours, O Rudra, which is auspicious, auspicious and always healing,
auspicious and healing of suffering—with that be gracious to us, that we may live.
May the missile of Rudra bypass us, may the ill-will of the furious, malevolent one bypass [us].
Unstring your stiff [bows] away from our patrons; O bountiful one, be gracious to our children and offspring.
Most bountiful one, most auspicious one, be auspicious and well-disposed to us.
Placing your weapon upon the highest tree, clad in a skin, go forth;
bearing the Pināka, go forth.
O scattering one, bright red one, homage be to you, O blessed one;
those thousands of missiles that are yours, let them strike down another than us.
A thousandfold are the thousands of missiles in your two arms;
O blessed one, being their master, make their faces turn away.
Innumerable thousands are the Rudras over the earth;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those Bhavas that are in this great billowing flood, the atmosphere;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those Rudras who are blue-necked, white-throated, having resorted to heaven;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those Śarvas who are blue-necked, white-throated, roaming below upon the earth;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those who in the woods are yellow as young grass, blue-necked, bright red;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those who pierce people in their foods and drinking vessels;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those who are overlords of spirits, hairless or braided-haired;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those protectors of paths who dwell on food and fight for grain;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those who wander about the fords, bearing darts and quivers;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those Rudras, so many or even more, who have spread across the quarters;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Homage be to the Rudras who are in heaven, whose arrows are rain:
to them ten [fingers extended] to the east, ten to the south, ten to the west, ten to the north, ten upwards;
homage be to them;
may they be gracious to us;
whomsoever we hate and whoever hates us, him I place in their jaws.
Homage be to the Rudras who are in the atmosphere, whose arrows are wind:
to them ten [fingers extended] to the east, ten to the south, ten to the west, ten to the north, ten upwards;
homage be to them;
may they be gracious to us;
whomsoever we hate and whoever hates us, him I place in their jaws.
Homage be to the Rudras who are on the earth, whose arrows are food:
to them ten [fingers extended] to the east, ten to the south, ten to the west, ten to the north, ten upwards;
homage be to them;
may they be gracious to us;
whomsoever we hate and whoever hates us, him I place in their jaws.
